<h2><strong>How Ignoring Screen-Related Eye Symptoms Makes Things Worse</strong></h2>
<p>Screens force your eyes to work differently than reading print or looking at distance. Over time, that extra effort can lead to accommodative stress, focus fatigue, and chronic discomfort.</p>
<p>Ignoring these symptoms doesn’t make them disappear. Without proper care, digital eye strain can affect productivity, sleep quality, and long-term visual comfort.</p>
<p>A computer vision exam identifies these issues early and helps prevent them from becoming persistent problems.</p>
<h2><strong>What to Expect From Computer Vision Exams</strong></h2>
<p>A<a href="{{ route('eye-vision-exams') }}"> computer vision exam</a> goes beyond a routine eye checkup. It evaluates how your eyes function during prolonged screen use, not just how clearly you see.</p>
<h3><strong>What Screen Use Does to Your Eyes</strong></h3>
<ul>
<li>Extended screen time often causes:</li>
<li>Reduced blink rate, leading to dry eyes after work</li>
<li>Vergence dysfunction, making it harder for eyes to work together</li>
<li>Accommodative stress, which affects near focusing</li>
<li>Blurred vision while reading screens</li>
<li>Headaches from computer use and visual fatigue</li>
</ul>
<p>Blue light exposure and screen flicker can also disrupt circadian rhythm and increase sensitivity to light.</p>
<h3><strong>What a Computer Vision Exam Evaluates</strong></h3>
<p>At Austin Optics, a screen time eye checkup may include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Assessment of focusing and eye teaming</li>
<li>Evaluation of viewing distance and angle</li>
<li>Screening for digital eye strain and screen-related headaches</li>
<li>Refraction for computer glasses prescriptions</li>
<li>Recommendations for anti-reflective coatings and blue light filtering</li>
</ul>
<p>Solutions may include occupational progressive lenses, office lenses vs. traditional progressives, variable tints like FL-41, or ergonomic vision adjustments.</p>
